type: software
title: "One noisy cellular automaton with an uncomputable noise diagram"
version: 1.2.0
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24147268"
date-released: '2026-11-12'
license: [CC-BY-4.0, MIT, Apache-2.0]
authors: [{"family-names": "Douglas", "given-names": "Seth", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"}, {"family-names": "Mghirbi", "given-names": "Nidhal", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6534-1118"}]
keywords: ["cellular automata", "probabilistic cellular automata", "noise", "ergodicity", "undecidability", "halting problem", "Gibbs measures", "noise diagram"]
abstract: "Can varying only the noise rate of one cellular automaton encode halting? We construct one computably specified finite-alphabet, finite-range automaton on Z^6 with computable disjoint rational open rate intervals I_n. Throughout I_n, uniform replacement noise makes it ergodic exactly when a fixed universal machine halts on n. Halting gives exponential all-input coalescence and attraction from every initial law; nonhalting gives at least two translation-invariant stationary laws and continuum many extremal stationary laws. Hierarchical rulers select the input from the noise rate. Finite computation boards read the input; a threshold checker preserves a bit, and interval sensors erase every surviving chart when the computation halts. The space-time correspondence gives one fixed {0,1}-energy interaction on Z^7: on the corresponding temperature windows, halting gives one Gibbs measure and exponential weak spatial mixing in arbitrary volumes; nonhalting gives continuum many extremal Gibbs measures. These extremes need not be translation invariant. The zero-field pressure is analytic, yet its derivative in one fixed field direction exists at zero exactly on halting windows. Thus one fixed local rule has an uncomputable noise diagram. Theorem A, the ergodicity criterion with attraction and two invariant laws, is verified in Lean."
